  "limitations": [
    "The study was only carried out in the summer, limiting generalizability to other seasons.",
    "The subjects were exclusively university students from one university, with a high level of education and good health, which limits the generalizability of results to broader populations.",
    "The study focused only on physiological indicators, and future research should include psychological and endocrine hormones for a more comprehensive understanding of environmental impact on health."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This empirical field study investigated the benefits of a three-day bamboo forest therapy session on the physiological responses and subjective environmental evaluations of 120 university students. Compared to an urban environment, bamboo forest sites significantly improved students' environmental experience across sensory, atmospheric, climatic, place, and space factors. The therapy session led to decreased blood pressure and heart rate, and increased peripheral oxygen saturation, with viewing activities having a more pronounced effect on heart rate reduction. Female subjects showed a more significant decrease in systolic blood pressure and heart rate compared to males. Limitations include the study being conducted only in summer, the use of a homogenous university student population, and the exclusive focus on physiological indicators, suggesting future research should incorporate seasonal variations, diverse demographics, and additional psychological and endocrine measures.",
